course: dictionary
lesson: 9123
title: Smooth: flat, even and easy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Smooth has more than one meaning, so look at the words around it to find the right one. «Deer avoid frozen ponds because hard hooves slip on smooth ice.» The word slip is the clue: things slide on smooth ice because it has no bumps or rough parts.
explain: Now compare «Grow a bean in a glass jar and you will see fine white fuzz near the root tip» with «like a towel with lots of loops soaking up more water than a smooth cloth.» A towel has loops and fuzz, but a smooth cloth does not. Here smooth means flat and even, with nothing sticking up.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Try «a flat, even cloth» in place of «a smooth cloth», and see if it still makes sense. It does, so you have found the meaning.
fact: «Smooth» means flat and even, with no rough parts, bumps or holes.
fact: «Smooth» can also describe a ride that moves without sudden jolts, or a liquid with no lumps.
fact: To smooth something means to make it flat or even with your hand.
fact: Smooth surfaces like glass and ice have less friction than rough ones like sandpaper, so things slide further on them.
fact: Rough surfaces make more friction than smooth surfaces.
exercise: «Deer avoid frozen ponds because hard hooves slip on smooth ice.» What does smooth mean here? | *Flat with no bumps, so things slide | Soft and warm | Covered in thick fur | Wet with rain
exercise: Which surface has less friction, so a box slides further on it? | Rough carpet | Sandpaper | *Glass | Brick wall
exercise: «Stir until smooth.» What does smooth mean in this recipe? | Very hot and steaming | *No lumps | Burnt and black | Crunchy with bits
test: «The ride was smooth, with no sudden jolts.» What does smooth mean? | Covered in ice | Bumpy and noisy | *Moving without sudden jolts | Very fast and wild
test: Dad asks you to smooth the sheet with your hand. What are you doing? | Cutting it into pieces | Folding it in half | Tearing it apart | *Making it flat and even
exam: Why are «worn-smooth» tyres dangerous in the rain? | They make a loud noise on the road | Their rubber is too heavy for the car | *They lose grip, so the car can skid on the water | They are too bright to see
